{
  "requirement": "Before presenting, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to apply the revised text the reviews return. Where reviews conflict, for example two reviews that split the same requirement differently, it MUST direct them to reconcile the conflict, and to present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_applies_and_reconciles_reviews_before_presenting]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It reads the bundled `habeas-requirements` skill, and each obligation in `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.present.apply` maps to an assertion:\n\n- **Apply the revised text the reviews return, before presenting.** `step_of(SKILL, \"Apply the revised text the reviews return\")` panics unless some workflow step contains that phrase. The final `assert!(apply < step_of(SKILL, \"Present each group to the user\"))` checks that this step comes before the presenting step.\n- **Reconcile conflicting reviews, such as two that split the same requirement differently.** `assert_text` on `step(SKILL, apply)` requires the phrase \"reconcile reviews that clash, such as two that split the same requirement differently\" in that step.\n- **Present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.** The same `assert_text` call requires \"a clash whose resolution changes what is promised becomes a design question\" in the apply step.\n\nThe helpers `step`, `step_of` and `assert_text` fail with panics when the step or phrase is missing, so the assertions are enforced. The requirement is about skill text, so these text-and-ordering checks suit it. I found no further distinct modes or boundaries that need separate tests.",
  "references": "7fcd06ca1a2872c27da2285910352723fcb23fa3573aa3937ecdbc4f3f42bfa4",
